task: dataflow-netem
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-12
branch: feature/dataflow-netem
worktree: ../okay-wt-dnm1
spec: specs/dataflow.md, stage 12 — the one-machine half

why: stage 12 proper needs machines that are not this one and is not
  pretended at. But one of its boxes was always answerable here: the
  tolerance `dataflow-reconnect` chose — three consecutive failures —
  was a judgement, and the question it begs is measurable without a
  wire: at what LOSS RATE does a run stop finishing?

what: a `Serve` that loses requests by a SEEDED schedule — a splitmix
  stream per worker, so a loss on one is not a loss on all — and a
  sweep over loss rates and seeds that counts runs finished, attempts
  lost and workers buried. Seeded, so it is deterministic: the same
  seed drops the same requests, a red run names the seed, and the
  sweep can live in the default gate rather than in Live.

  Two claims the sweep makes and the test asserts: a lossless wire
  always finishes, and tolerance 3 carries a 10% loss rate over forty
  schedules. Everything above that is a TABLE the spec records —
  where finishing stops being certain is the number this lane exists
  to produce, and it is not guessed at before the run.

what it does not model, said in the file: latency (a lost request
  costs no time here), partial delivery, and a partition of the
  network. All three are stage 12's and need the machines.

predict: a 10% loss holds trivially and the knee is somewhere between
  30% and 50%, because three consecutive losses on one worker at rate
  p is p^3 per attempt and eight partitions give a run few enough
  attempts that even 0.5^3 rarely lands three in a row — until the
  survivors thin out. If the knee is lower than that, the interesting
  question becomes whether the count should be per WORKER (as it is)
  or per PARTITION.
